00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.510
It can often involve offensive contact.

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:44.720
And if a regular customer
would not be okay

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.900
with being gassed with
some sort of sedative,

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:49.080
that probably qualifies.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:50.750
Carl's Jr. of the future

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:53.350
engaging in some questionable practices.

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:54.930
- [Automated Voice] Welcome to Carl's Jr.

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:58.950
Would you like to try
our extra big ass taco

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:00.627
now with more molecules?

00:02:01.891 --> 00:02:02.724
- Hey!

00:02:02.724 --> 00:02:04.940
Is this particular
individual the unfit mother?

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:05.940
- Me? No.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:09.023
- Okay, this particular
individual is unscannable.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.100
- So obviously Luke Wilson
is not an unfit mother,

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.130
but I think this does raise the question

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:21.430
of when are the police
actually allowed to arrest you?

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:22.950
And under the fourth amendment

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.760
police are authorized to make an arrest

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:26.570
so long as they have probable cause.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:28.560
And in legalese we say probable cause

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.060
must be based on specific
articulable facts

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.130
taken together with rational inferences

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:34.230
from those other facts.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:38.690
So here you cannot make a
reasonable inference from a kiosk

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:40.720
an automated kiosk, a robot effectively,

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.440
saying that Luke Wilson,
obviously not an unfit mother,

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.180
is an unfit mother.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:47.760
Here, not surprisingly,

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.210
these police do not have probable cause

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:52.000
to arrest Luke Wilson.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.800
But by the same token most courts say

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:56.190
that if police mistakenly arrest someone

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:57.830
due to a misidentification,

00:02:57.830 --> 00:02:59.680
then there's no constitutional violation

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:01.570
so long as that mistake was reasonable.

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:03.410
So it's a question here

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:06.280
whether the police were
acting reasonably or not.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:07.740
Probably not.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:08.573
And as to the question

00:03:08.573 --> 00:03:10.500
of whether the police
can just tackle someone

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.100
during an arrest,

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:14.720
police are generally allowed
to use reasonable force

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:17.680
to restrain and arrest someone,

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.560
assuming that they have a probable cause

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:22.610
to be able to arrest
someone in the first place.

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:25.080
But I for one find it
completely unrealistic

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:26.920
that any police officers
in the United States

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:29.120
would ever use unreasonable force

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:32.760
to detain and then take
someone into custody.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:33.920
That just,

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:35.073
that doesn't happen.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:39.113
- Wait a second.

00:03:39.113 --> 00:03:40.890
There's the other pod
from the army experiment.

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:42.210
There was a girl, she was
from the same experiment.

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:44.591
- Yeah, that's enough
of your bullshit, sir.

00:03:44.591 --> 00:03:46.360
(Joe yelling)

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:47.390
- [Narrator] Joe was arrested

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:49.090
for not paying his hospital bill--

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:52.898
- Okay, so Joe is arrested,

00:03:52.898 --> 00:03:57.050
obviously in a situation
that's completely unreasonable.

00:03:57.050 --> 00:03:58.470
Then he's in the back of the squad car

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:00.870
and they mace him for asking questions.

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:01.800
Police,

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.180
when they have someone in custody

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:06.380
and they have someone
that is resisting arrest

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:08.470
or attacking an officer,

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:11.740
are allowed to increase
the force that they use

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:13.030
to counter the resistance.

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:14.970
But it's an unreasonable use of force

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:17.600
to mace someone who is handcuffed

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.970
and is not threatening the officers.

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:22.420
But again, this is completely unrealistic.

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:24.060
We never see police officers

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:27.580
just indiscriminately
using mace or pepper spray

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:30.060
against people who were
not resisting arrest

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:31.600
or just going about their day

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:33.210
exercising their first amendment rights

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:35.760
to freedom of expression.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.780
- Now since y'all say
you aint got no money

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:43.520
we have proprietarily obtained for you

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:46.800
one of them court appointed lawyers.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.150
- Okay, so that is
absolutely a real thing.

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:50.790
There are court appointed lawyers,

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.090
they're called public defenders.

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:53.640
There was actually a 1963 case

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:55.230
called Gideon versus Wainwright

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:56.790
that established the constitutional right

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:59.710
to people who are accused of criminal acts

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:01.040
who can't afford an attorney

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:02.970
because it would be such an injustice

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:04.790
to deny them an attorney

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:06.980
to represent them in this criminal trial.

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:09.810
Then the state can and should provide them

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:10.750
with a public defender

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:12.560
to at least provide some defense.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:14.500
Though we've seen in a lot
of different situations

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:16.820
where some states seemingly on purpose

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:19.270
will underfund the
public defender's office.

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:21.650
We've seen allegations
of that in Louisiana

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:23.080
where often the district attorney

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:25.490
gets millions of dollars per year

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:28.070
but the public defender's
office will get nothing

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:30.483
and they're not able
to provide the defense

00:05:30.483 --> 00:05:33.190
that accused are
constitutionally entitled to.

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:34.670
- So put your hands together

00:05:34.670 --> 00:05:36.740
and give it up for Frito Pendejo.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:41.450
- You're my lawyer?

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:43.623
- A lawyer with a corporate sponsorship?

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:46.410
That's completely ridiculous.

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:49.330
Legal Eagle is sponsored by
CuriosityStream and Nebula.

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:52.440
I mean here the whole courtroom
has various corporate logos

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:54.360
completely plastered over everything.

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:56.960
There's a flag that has a corporate logo,

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.510
it appears to be funding
the criminal justice system.

00:05:59.510 --> 00:06:01.500
Often people talk about Judge Judy

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:05.140
or the TV shows that you see
that have an actual real judge.

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:07.330
It's actually private arbitration

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:08.750
where the contestants agree

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:12.120
to be bound by the decision of Judge Judy

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:13.940
or whoever is playing the judge.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:15.670
But it's still not a real courtroom.

00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:17.510
It would be completely ridiculous

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:20.570
for the actual state courtroom

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:22.190
to have corporate sponsorship.

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:24.370
And you can imagine that
there'll be all kinds of reasons

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:26.220
why you wouldn't want private enterprise

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:30.370
to interfere with the criminal
justice system itself.

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:32.170
- [Judge] Prosecutor.

00:06:32.170 --> 00:06:33.460
Why do you think he done it?

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:35.730
- 'Kay, number one, your honor,

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:39.250
just look at him.
(crowd laughing)

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:42.330
- I assume that this is some
sort of opening statement here.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:44.400
You're not allowed to
actually make an argument

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:45.450
in opening statements,

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:47.370
that's a common misconception.

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:49.510
You're supposed to say what
the evidence is going to show.

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:51.650
Really seems like they're not
going to enter any evidence,

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:53.260
it just seems like they're gonna go

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:55.300
with whatever the prosecutor says here.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:58.470
- We've got all this evidence

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:02.030
of how this guy didn't
even pay at the hospital.

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:05.360
And I heard that he doesn't
even have his tattoo.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:07.060
- Okay, I stand corrected.

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:09.270
He is now saying that he has evidence

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:10.920
that's going to show something.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:13.540
He skipped out on some
sort of hospital bill.

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:16.990
Why that would be a criminal
issue I'm not entirely sure.

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:19.640
And then he lapses into hearsay,

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:20.990
saying that he heard

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:24.070
that he doesn't have the scannable tattoo.

00:07:24.070 --> 00:07:26.560
No idea why this would be the place

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:28.140
where he'd rely on hearsay evidence

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:31.550
because he'd just looked
at the wrist of the accused

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:34.700
and find out whether he
actually has the tattoo or not.

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:37.400
Though as we've talked a
lot about on this channel,

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:38.950
the exceptions to the hearsay rule

00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:41.150
really swallow the hearsay rule in general

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:44.490
so that usually if you
have hearsay evidence

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:47.280
you can find a way to get
it admissible into court.

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:49.910
The way he has raised at here
might be the rare exception

00:07:49.910 --> 00:07:51.670
where that hearsay is not coming in

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:54.220
because there's lots of
other kinds of evidence

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:55.053
that would be better.

00:07:55.053 --> 00:07:58.840
It seems like in the future
the courts are not great.

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:02.000
- I know, and I'm all
"You gotta be (bleep) me."

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:03.440
But check this out, man.

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:05.970
Joe should be like guilty.

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:08.420
Peace.
(crowd cheering)

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:10.730
- Honestly, that is not
the worst opening statement

00:08:10.730 --> 00:08:12.530
I've ever heard in my life.

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:14.170
(crowd cheering)
- Objection.

00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:16.550
- What are you objectifying on?

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:17.850
- It's too late to object.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:22.053
- Your honor,

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:23.843
I object

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:28.800
that this guy also broke my --.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:30.182
Yeah.

00:08:30.182 --> 00:08:31.190
- What?

00:08:31.190 --> 00:08:32.410
- And you know what else?

00:08:32.410 --> 00:08:35.800
I object that he's not gonna
have any money to pay me

00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:39.500
after he pays back all the money
he stole from the hospital.

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:42.000
- Okay, lots going on here, lots wrong.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:42.833
First of all,

00:08:42.833 --> 00:08:46.060
if the public defender is a
material witness to the case

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:48.000
then they'd be conflicted out.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:50.060
There's no way that they could represent

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:51.190
this particular individual.

00:08:51.190 --> 00:08:53.270
And of course they'd probably
be called to the stand

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:55.100
if they're actually ever going to ask

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:56.360
for evidence to be submitted.

00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:57.193
On top of that,

00:08:57.193 --> 00:08:58.026
it is a big problem

00:08:58.026 --> 00:09:00.730
in both the criminal
and civil justice system

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:03.070
that a lot of people aren't able to afford

00:09:03.070 --> 00:09:05.040
the verdicts that are
entered against them.

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:06.790
That's being called judgment proof.

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:08.090
The judgment is effectively invalid

00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:09.230
because they'll never be able to pay

00:09:09.230 --> 00:09:12.800
the things that they stole or
are liable to somebody for.

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:13.710
That's a big issue,

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:15.630
but as a public defender
you're not allowed

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:18.790
to raise that as a defense
in the case itself.

00:09:18.790 --> 00:09:20.230
I mean there's already a lot of stuff here

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:22.480
that would qualify for a mistrial.

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:24.050
- [Joe] What are we doing here?

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:26.650
- Okay, sir, we're engaged
in procuring your tattoo.

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:30.673
- [Automated Voice] Welcome

00:09:30.673 --> 00:09:33.590
to the Identity Processing
Program of America.

00:09:33.590 --> 00:09:37.323
Please insert your forearm
into the forearm receptacle.

00:09:39.311 --> 00:09:40.420
Thank you.

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:43.700
- Okay, it should come as a
surprise to absolutely no one

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:46.850
that the government can't
force you to get in ID,

00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:48.370
though there are plenty of situations

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:50.220
where a government actor

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:52.600
can force you to produce an identification

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:55.170
that you already have
if you have it on you.

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:58.460
Though forcing you to get
a tattoo on your body,

00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:00.330
almost certainly unconstitutional.

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:03.060
Though there are certain instances where

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:04.500
a government can force you

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:06.550
to undergo certain bodily modifications

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:07.450
if it's in the interest

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:10.580
of health, safety, welfare,
or the morals of society.

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:13.790
And that sounds farfetched
and it sounds draconian,

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:16.000
but we saw an example
of that very recently

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:18.100
when we've been talking about vaccines,

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:21.840
because in the 1905 case of
Jacobson versus Massachusetts

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:24.030
the Supreme court ruled seven to two

00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:25.960
that a mandatory vaccination law

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:28.110
was legitimate exercise of state power

00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:29.870
to protect public health and safety.

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:32.210
In terms of the spectrum
of what's allowable

00:10:32.210 --> 00:10:35.010
between a vaccination and a forced tattoo,

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:37.260
maybe there's a big
chasm between those two

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:39.130
and maybe they're not that far away.

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:43.440
It sort of depends on the
justification and the exercise

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:46.590
and the reason for the exercise
of the state's police power.

00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:48.010
- Yeah, I don't see you in here.

00:10:48.010 --> 00:10:51.320
So you're gonna have to stay in prison.

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:52.630
- Could you check again,

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:56.290
'cause I was definitely in prison, okay.

00:10:56.290 --> 00:10:58.480
I got sat on my face and everything.

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:00.433
Maybe check those files back there.

00:11:02.504 --> 00:11:05.087
(upbeat music)

00:11:06.887 --> 00:11:10.657
- [Automated Voice]
Escape, escape, escape.

00:11:10.657 --> 00:11:15.657
(automatic gunfire)
(alarm sounding)

00:11:17.180 --> 00:11:18.930
- Okay, so the question here is

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:20.290
can you use deadly force

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:22.980
against a convict who
is attempting to escape?

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:24.730
And the answer generally is yes.

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:27.330
States like Florida give
officers the legal right

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:29.640
to use any force that they
believe reasonably necessary

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:31.990
to prevent the escape
of an arrested person.

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:33.760
And not a single state

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:36.430
requires the use of deadly
force as a last resort.

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:38.600
Now the American Bar
Association has urged states

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:40.560
to allow deadly force only when necessary

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:43.180
to prevent an escape by a prisoner

00:11:43.180 --> 00:11:45.930
when the person is about
to leave the enclosed area

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:47.100
without authorization,

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:48.590
and the ABA also recommends

00:11:48.590 --> 00:11:50.260
that the officers shout a verbal warning

00:11:50.260 --> 00:11:53.410
before attempting to
shoot or use deadly force.

00:11:53.410 --> 00:11:55.519
But generally that's not required.

00:11:55.519 --> 00:11:56.730
- Oh yeah.

00:11:56.730 --> 00:11:58.702
